209
00:16:22,623 --> 00:16:25,570
enough, in terms of
commits, trees and blobs.

210
00:16:27,410 --> 00:16:28,337
Let's land this.

211
00:16:28,677 --> 00:16:30,337
You should now be able to say:

212
00:16:30,677 --> 00:16:35,374
Blobless plus cone: all commits and trees
are local, and blobs are fetched only for

213
00:16:35,414 --> 00:16:36,687
the cone and on demand.

214
00:16:37,027 --> 00:16:41,233
scalar clone is a partial clone, a
cone, a set of settings and background

215
00:16:41,273 --> 00:16:46,062
maintenance; it also registers the enlistment
globally and starts a monitor daemon.

216
00:16:46,402 --> 00:16:51,532
Affected projects come from a three-dot
diff, which needs the merge base; a blobless

217
00:16:51,572 --> 00:16:53,932
clone has it, a shallow clone does not.

218
00:16:54,272 --> 00:16:59,674
Tags are repository-wide; prefix them per
project and use git describe --match.

219
00:17:00,014 --> 00:17:04,831
CODEOWNERS is GitHub's path-based ownership;
it requests reviews and is read from

220
00:17:04,871 --> 00:17:05,790
the base branch.

221
00:17:07,630 --> 00:17:15,723
Read sections 24.7 to 24.14 of Chapter
24 and do the Practice section 24.16.

222
00:17:16,063 --> 00:17:23,177
That closes scale. You can now say which
objects a question needs before you choose

223
00:17:23,217 --> 00:17:29,814
a clone, and that's real progress.
Practise it on one repository of your own.

224
00:17:29,854 --> 00:17:36,967
Next time: the gate briefing for internals.
Until then, look at the state first and

225
00:17:37,007 --> 00:17:40,156
type second. See you in the next one.
